<!--
    Conditional execution: an op with no readers in a heritaged space.

    ConditionalExecution (condexe.rs, ported from decompiler/cpp/condexe.cc)
    removes a block that re-tests a condition its dominator already decided.
    An op in that block whose output has no readers may go only once its
    space has had a heritage pass (buildHeritageArray). kuna hard-wired that
    array to false, so such a block was never removed.

    Authored x86-64 (nasm):

        test edi,edi ; je L1 ; mov dword [rsi],1
    L1: test edi,edi ; je L2 ; mov dword [rsi+4],2
    L2: ret

    `deadcode delay register 50` keeps the unread flag writes of the second
    `test` alive past dead-code removal, so they reach the removability test.

    Fixed: both stores sit under one `if (x)`.
    Stub (before): `if (x) *p = 1; ... if (x) p[1] = 2;` - two tests.

-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
85ff7406c70601000000c74424f80500000085ff7407c7460402000000c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void f(int4 x, int4 *p);</com>
  <com>deadcode delay register 50</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CONDEXE #1: the re-test is removed, one if guards both stores" min="1" max="1">if \(x\) \{</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CONDEXE #2: no second test of x" min="1" max="1">if \(x\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CONDEXE #3: no unbraced single-store if remains" min="0" max="0">if \(x\)$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
